# CODEOWNERS — a notification and review control, NOT access control.
#
# Listing a path here does not restrict who may change it. It requests a review from the named
# owner and, with branch protection's "require review from Code Owners" enabled, holds the merge
# until that review arrives. Anyone with write access can still open the PR and write the code.
#
# Today every owner below is the same small group of people, so this file buys the notification and
# the pause, not the gatekeeping (CONTRIBUTING.md, "Branching and merging"). Do not read the list
# as a permissions boundary — permissions live in the repository's collaborator and
# branch-protection settings.
#
# ORDERING: in CODEOWNERS the LAST matching pattern wins — unlike .gitignore, matches do not
# accumulate. Broad patterns therefore come first and narrow ones last. A broad pattern added at
# the bottom of this file silently unowns every specific path above it.
#
# Paths carry a leading `/` so they anchor at the repository root.
#
# Why each entry is here: CONTRIBUTING.md:48 (owned paths), CONTRIBUTING.md:85 (the licence
# firewall), docs/adr/0009-apache-2-0-and-dco.md:31, docs/adr/0010-agpl-clean-room-firewall.md:42.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broad — the machinery that enforces every other rule in the repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CI, the action pins, the issue and PR templates, and this file itself. "Do not edit
# .github/workflows/ to make a job green" (CONTRIBUTING.md) is only reviewable if someone is asked
# to look.
/.github/                                   @prokopto-dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Narrow — single files and leaf directories where a plausible-looking wrong change is expensive
# and hard to spot in review.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The agent contract and the normative conventions document. These two win over every other
# document, so a change here silently re-aims every rule in the repository.
/AGENTS.md                                  @prokopto-dev
/docs/design/00-canonical-conventions.md    @prokopto-dev

# Licence and trademark. Apache-2.0 with a DCO and no CLA cannot be changed without every past
# contributor (ADR-0009); NOTICE carries the §4 attribution obligations; the name and logo are
# deliberately not licensed. ADR-0009:31 and ADR-0010:42 both name these three.
/LICENSE                                    @prokopto-dev
/NOTICE                                     @prokopto-dev
/TRADEMARK.md                               @prokopto-dev

# Highest blast radius in the repo: the append-only ledger writer plus the invariant engine, and
# the pure point strategies. A wrong rounding or a lost invariant is a guild losing points its
# members earned, and it does not look wrong in a diff read quickly.
/internal/ledger/                           @prokopto-dev
/internal/strategy/                         @prokopto-dev

# Sources of truth for generated artefacts. schema.hcl drives every migration Atlas writes;
# openapi.json is the published API contract and its operationIds are permanent public surface.
/db/schema.hcl                              @prokopto-dev
/openapi/openapi.json                       @prokopto-dev

# The `!breaking-api` label is author-settable, so on its own it is only a "did you notice"
# control. Ownership of the changelog is what turns it into a review control — see the
# `api / breaking-change` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml.
/docs/api-changelog.md                      @prokopto-dev

# Expected outputs. Rewriting a golden file or a fixture is the standard way to turn a red build
# green while destroying the evidence that it was ever red.
/test/golden/                               @prokopto-dev
/test/fixtures/                             @prokopto-dev

# The SPA bundle-size budget is a one-way ratchet: raising max_gzip_bytes to land an oversized
# bundle requires a review here, turning a size regression into a decision in the diff.
/web/bundle-budget.json                     @prokopto-dev
